\f2\i0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The ground cab could deliver Hayley only as close as the corner of the block of the bombing. Farther ahead, it seemed an area around the site was still cordoned off. Passage down the street was blocked. Hayley could see the tops of several khaki tents. A she got closer, the pavement under her feet became more scorched. The surface of the road was pot-holed and uneven. The closer Hayley neared ground zero, the road was torched concrete with only a few clumps of bitumen lumped, melted and swollen. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley could hear music, and the buzz of a happy crowd underneath. what seemed to be a crowd of gawkers standing over the cordon lines turned out to be a queue, waiting for a bouncer to check their identity and admit them through. Hayley flashed her badge.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Welcome to the 
\f1\i Blow Shit Up Lounge
\f2\i0 ,\'92 the bouncer said. \'91The bar\'92s in the tent on the left. Fireworks in the tent on the right.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley nearly leapt into the bouncers arms as a pair of smoking spinning catherine wheels landed nearby and nearly deafened her. The next thing she heard was a trio of boozers laughing from some picnic chairs surrounding a card table laden with a new shout of beers and an ample supply of fireworks.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Excuse me,\'92 Hayley asked as she eyed off the fireworks on the table, scattered among the next round of full glasses. \'91Do you know Sukrit Supaporn?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Who luv?\'92 one boozer asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Are you his date?\'92 asked another warily.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Um, no,\'92 Hayley said, considered her next move, and decided to hold off showing her badge. \'91I\'92m just looking for Sukrit Supaporn.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We don\'92t know any 
\f1\i Secreet
\f2\i0  Supaporn,\'92 said the first boozer then, with sly looks and a crooked smile, shared a private joke with his buddies.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91He goes by the nickname Tony,\'92 Hayley prompted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, 
